WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
*slurp*

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.560
Mmmm, still good.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:12.600
Hey, everyone. What's happenin' ? It's mayuko. Thank you so much to the love on the last two videos.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.500
Every time I saw a comment about how excited you all are about tech and software engineering

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.960
it made me soo happy.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.480
So many of you asked me questions about how I got into tech and software engineering

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.240
so today, I'm going to dive in and tell you my story.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.320
Here we go!

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.680
I never coded before college. My high school never offered computer science classes

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.340
and I never thought that I would be a software engineer, ever.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.900
I went to college at UC San Diego where I started my freshman year as undeclared.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.340
I actually was considering being like a chemistry or neuroscience major

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:50.180
But I took an intro course in each and I quickly noped out of there because I realized it wasn't for me.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.800
I was hanging out with my dorm friends one day when one of them suggested I try computer science.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.760
He was a CS major himself and he said that with my logically minded brain

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.160
and my desire to do something sciency, computer science would be perfect for me!

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.600
He also mentioned that there's more software engineering jobs than there are software engineers

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.460
and as someone who really wanted to have job security and be financially stable right after college

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.580
that sounded really awesome!

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.540
At this point in my freshman year

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:23.399
I was basically willing to try anything because of my two failed attempts at declaring my major previously.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.260
To find out a little bit more about computer science, I went to the campus library to do some research

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:34.460
and by research, I mean check out any books that have the word computers or software in them

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.880
or anything that had code.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.799
I flipped through them to see if I would enjoy absorbing the information that was presented in these books.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:47.160
I wasn't exactly like, "Oh my gosh, linked lists are like the coolest thing, how did no one tell me about them?"

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:53.080
But I was having a much easier time absorbing the information than I did my chemistry and bio classes.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.360
Literally the next day I signed up for my intro to computer science class.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.660
And after getting through that class and being like "that wasn't too bad, I think I can do more"

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.880
I declared my major as computer science!

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.480
After taking a couple more classes and becoming friends with my classmates and tutors

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.240
it became clear to me that internships are pivotal in getting a job after college.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:20.540
Now to preface, I worked part-time jobs all through college to support myself financially

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.020
and through my freshman year, I worked at Jamba Juice and the campus bookstore.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.660
In my second year, I found a job at UCSD Department of Biomedical Informatics.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:33.340
They were a department that uses data analytics to sequence genes to help save people.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.180
Pretty cool stuff!

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.820
I started working there as an office assistant being paid close to minimum wage,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.680
helping organize files and folders, and also helping with the conferences that the division put on.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.160
I knew they had a summer internship program, so I was hoping this job would serve as a foot in the door to that program.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.430
When the internship program started accepting applications,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.030
I want to go talk to the program lead, and he gave me a spot in the program,

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.720
thanks to my foot-in-the-door technique.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.780
My project there was to create a webpage that would help scientists visualize the data that they're getting.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.120
I used a lot of JavaScript frameworks and PHP.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.640
Looking back on it, I literally had a 2,000 line PHP file because I didn't really know what I was doing.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:17.280
But that's okay because I learned a ton about software development!

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.920
After my three-month internship ended,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.180
I gained some confidence in that I could actually build something that people would use,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.520
and so now I wanted to use my skills to something that I was more personally interested in.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.980
At the time, I was watching a lot of Doctor Who and Neil Degrasse Tyson and Carl Sagan,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.780
and so my next endeavor was going to be space.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.660
My friend actually had an internship on campus that was run by the Sally Ride foundation.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.220
Sally Ride was the first American woman in space and her company was hiring!

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.920
I went to the typical interview process where I

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.760
talked through my resume and talked about different projects that I worked on

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.060
and I got the job!

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.540
I think it was actually one of my coolest jobs to date.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:03.820
Not only was I finally being able to use my coding skills for something that I was interested in,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:11.500
but we had this cool mission control room where we ran "missions" for this project called Grail MoonKAM.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:16.620
There I made a Java-based game intended to teach middle school students how the Grail spacecraft worked

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:22.740
It was a lot of object-oriented programming and learning how to use Java's rendering library: Java 2D swing.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.920
This job was also great because I could work during the school year part-time while I was continuing my studies.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.760
Now summer was approaching quickly and at this point, I was in my third year of college.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.040
I really wanted some experience working at a big tech company,

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:39.520
since I set my eyes on working at one right after I graduated.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.200
I went to the tech job fair on campus and faced rejection after rejection,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.460
from I'm handing out all my resumes.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.840
After lots of applying and interviewing at tech companies

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.420
and also applying through my friends who worked at them, I got an internship at Intuit.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.940
a financial software company that makes Turbotax.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:03.980
During my 3 month internship there, I worked on Turbotax.com and used a lot of JavaScript and HTML

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.200
and I learned a lot about software development and its processes.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.540
and after that was done, I went back to school for my fourth and final year of college.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:18.020
I successfully graduated from UCSD in 2014 with my Bachelor's in Computer Science.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:20.020
*yayy!!*

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.400
Now, to take a step back,

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.620
and I know I'm really fortunate to say this, but college is one of the hardest things that I've ever done.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.660
I worked part-time to support myself financially,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.020
and I was also a part of a lot of student organizations to network

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.500
and also have a community of friends who were also studying engineering.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:41.120
Balancing all of that with a demanding computer science curriculum was really tough,

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:45.800
but I somehow made it through and it's still one of my proudest accomplishments to date!

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.620
I rejoined Intuit after I graduated,

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:53.920
and was part of a two-year rotational program that would give me the opportunity to rotate between

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.500
different teams and projects and products and work on different technologies.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.020
especially because I didn't really know whether I wanted to work on web, or mobile,

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.040
or backend or infra [infrastructure] stuff quite yet.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.020
It just so happened that my first rotation was on the Turbotax iOS team,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.460
where I had my very first introduction to mobile programming, which I still do today.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:18.700
I was fortunate enough to join an awesome team that showed me how fun work can be

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:23.620
and how rewarding it is to work on a project that has a positive impact on people's lives.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:30.060
When my rotation ended, I decided I wanted to move up to Silicon Valley to be fully embedded in the tech world

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.240
and also experience what it's like to be a software engineer working in the Valley.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:39.240
I thought maybe this was like Hollywood for actors or like Broadway for musical stars,

00:06:39.249 --> 00:06:40.840
so I was really excited!

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:46.920
I felt so excited to be taking such a big step in my career, and dive head-first into the tech world.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:50.340
I still worked at Intuit, but I worked from their Bay area office,

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.760
and I was still doing mobile development.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:56.180
After my rotation ended and writing a lot of Swift code,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.540
I decided that I wanted to write something other than financial software.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:05.100
I just so happened to stumble upon a job opening at Patreon

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.740
a company that creates a membership platform that makes it easy for creators

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:12.260
to get paid via membership tools and recurring funding from their fans.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:17.260
Since I've always loved the YouTube community and always loved to music growing up,

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:21.300
I saw this and I thought, "I have to have this job!"

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.560
And so I submitted my resume that same day.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.200
I interviewed with the typical three-stage interview process:

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:32.100
the phone screen, the technical phone screen or take-home project, and the on-site.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.100
And! I got the job!

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:34.880
*yay!*

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:41.040
This felt like getting my dream job and everything I had done led up to this point and this job.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.040
*fast forward*

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:49.779
Fast-forward to now, I still work at Patreon as an iOS engineer helping to build Patreon's iOS apps.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.300
I absolutely love what I do,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.420
and it's awesome to be a part of a project that helps fund the emerging creative class.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:58.540
So that's it!

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:04.880
That's been my career path so far. It's been highlighted with a lot of hard work, a lot of asking myself,

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:09.400
"What would make me happy to apply my skills to", and constant change.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:14.880
And I'm really proud of how far I've come. Needless to say there are so many different

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.420
paths and ways of getting into software engineering that are so different from my own.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:24.280
Several of my friends have had completely different journeys into software engineering

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:28.080
that are different from my own, and they're all achieving their goals and dreams.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:32.760
Mine is just one data point that I hope will help you somehow.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.140
There isn't one right way to get into software engineering!

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.120
So go with your gut and do what you're passionate about.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.240
Thank you so much for watching again.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:48.260
Thank you to every single one of you who has commented or reached out to me on my last two videos.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:54.640
Like I said in my previous video, my intent is to hopefully help foster a interest or career in technology,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.060
and so it really means so much to me that you all are enjoying this content.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:04.700
If you have any questions for me at all, please don't hesitate to leave a comment down below or

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.569
reach out to me via Twitter or Instagram, and if you like this video

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:12.650
please give it a thumbs up and subscribe to my channel if you'd like to see more!

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.949
Anyways, I hope you all have a great day!

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:18.679
I wonder how level my head is... oh-

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.370
It's like a game!

